**Headline read.** The status-quo surveillance system already costs roughly **R2,833,206/yr** in personnel + pesticide-allocated system spend across NMC, PIH, and NHLS BChE — borne almost entirely by NDoH, NHLS, and Stellenbosch FoMHS rather than appearing as a line item. Against that base, the **Surveillance MVP (S1 + S2) adds only R137,000/yr** — a **4.8% uplift on current spend** for a **+14.1 pp** jump in P_detect.

## 8. Implementation considerations

| Component | Lead agency | Year-1 cost | Critical path |
|:----------|:------------|------------:|:--------------|
| S1 PIH dashboard | PIH (Tygerberg) + NICD | 82,000 | Daily extract + hosting; MVD workshop |
| S2 BChE auto-notify | NHLS LIS team | 55,000 | LIS rule configuration; QA review |
| S3 Sentinel toxicology | NHLS clinical chemistry + sentinel hospital | R1,200,000 pilot | Lab method validation; sample logistics |
| S1b Phase 2 community layer *(pilot: FS + Gauteng; commission after Month 6 MVP review)* | NICD + provincial DoH EHPs | Pilot: R1M setup + R0.8M/yr; national: R3.5M setup + R2.5M/yr | CHW two-tier triage training, mobile checklist app, EHP call-back protocol, QA pipeline |

**Privacy & legal.** The PIH-to-NMC MVD feed transmits personal health information (age-band, sex, geography, clinical data) and must use a **secure, authenticated HTTPS/TLS API** — no plaintext or email transmission. De-identification before transmission is mandatory (age-band not exact age; province/district not street address; no name or phone number; see Appendix A2). The NHLS BChE auto-notify feed transmits result-level data with patient identifiers to NMC, consistent with the existing NMC notification pathway, and must use the same secure HL7-over-TLS channel. Both flows are POPIA-compliant under existing Section 90 NHA authority. **No new legislation required.**

**Risks.** (i) Low BChE testing uptake by clinicians — S2 fires only on tests ordered. (ii) Data-quality drift if facility metadata is incomplete. (iii) Capacity bottleneck at provincial response level — the [Coordination brief](coordination_policy.qmd) addresses this.

## 9. Recommended next step

**Adopt the Surveillance MVP (S1 + S2) and pilot in 3 provinces (Gauteng, Western Cape, KZN) for 6 months.** Approximate pilot budget: **~R137,000 operating** (NHLS–NMC IT feed is now operational). Lead agency: NICD as national coordinator.

- **Weeks 1–2:** S1 PIH dashboard live; S2 BChE LIS configuration begins.
- **Months 1–3:** S1 MVD workshop with PIH finalised; S2 BChE auto-notify QA review completed.
- **Month 6:** Evaluate against (a) completeness of NHLS severe results in NMC and (b) signal-to-investigation latency.
- **Month 9:** Add S3 sentinel toxicology site, funded from a separate research-surveillance stream.
- **Month 12:** Review MVP geographic distribution. If ≥40% of severe cases originate in low-facility-access districts, commission the S1b Phase 2 pilot (Free State + Gauteng; ~R1M setup + R0.8M/yr operating).

The coordination side of the same system — how NICD, EHPs, DALRRD, and provincial response teams act on the improved signal — is covered in the [Coordination brief](coordination_policy.qmd). The product-specific regulatory action that S3 makes possible is the subject of the [Terbufos brief](terbufos_policy.qmd).

